  realLife: [
    { tag: 'OVERWORK', tagZh: '过劳', verseRef: 'Proverbs 23:4',
      titleZh: '为“多一点”耗尽自己', titleEn: 'Wearing yourself out for “a little more”',
      bodyZh: '“不要劳碌求富……因[钱财]必长翅膀，如鹰向天飞去”（23:4–5）。这句古老的话精准地描述了现代人的一种疲惫：为了收入、为了更好的生活，我们把自己推到极限，牺牲睡眠、健康、家人的陪伴、与神的时间。经文并不否定努力工作，它反对的是“耗尽自己去追一个靠不住的目标”。财富说走就走，而你搭上的那些东西，未必还能要回来。智慧懂得在“够了”的地方停下来。',
      bodyEn: '“Don’t weary yourself to be rich… for wealth certainly sprouts wings and flies away like an eagle” (23:4–5). This ancient line captures a very modern exhaustion: to earn more, to live better, we push ourselves to the edge, trading away sleep, health, presence with family, time with God. The verse doesn’t oppose hard work; it opposes exhausting yourself for a goal that can’t be trusted to stay. Wealth can vanish overnight, and what you traded for it may not come back. Wisdom knows how to stop at “enough.”' },
    { tag: 'ENVY', tagZh: '嫉妒', verseRef: 'Proverbs 23:17',
      titleZh: '当“坏人”看起来过得更好', titleEn: 'When the “bad guys” seem to do better',
      bodyEn: 'Sometimes the people who cut corners, bend the rules, or ignore their conscience seem to end up richer, freer, more admired — and a quiet envy creeps in: why bother doing right? Proverbs 23:17–18 meets that honestly. It doesn’t deny the feeling; it redirects the gaze: “fear the LORD all day long… there is a future hope.” The story isn’t finished. What looks like winning may not be, and God safeguards the future of those who keep the right path. The antidote to envy isn’t willpower; it’s remembering how the story ends.',
      bodyZh: '有时候，那些走捷径、钻空子、无视良心的人，看起来反而更有钱、更自由、更受人羡慕——于是一种隐隐的嫉妒悄悄爬上来：那我认真做对的事，图什么呢？箴言 23:17–18 很诚实地回应了这种心情。它没有否认这感受，而是把我们的眼光转向别处：“只要终日敬畏耶和华……至终必有善报。”故事还没写完。看似赢的，未必真赢；而神会看顾那些持守正道之人的将来。对付嫉妒的解药不是咬牙硬撑，而是记得这故事将怎样收场。' },
    { tag: 'DISCIPLINE', tagZh: '管教', verseRef: 'Proverbs 23:13',
      titleZh: '爱，有时意味着不放任', titleEn: 'Love sometimes means not looking away',
      bodyZh: '箴言 23:13–14 说，不可因疼爱就不管教孩子，合宜的管教反而是在救他。在一个把“管教”与“伤害”很容易划等号的时代，这需要谨慎地理解：圣经从不认可虐待或以愤怒发泄式地对待孩子；它讲的是出于爱、有节制、有原则的引导。真正的爱不是让孩子想做什么就做什么，而是愿意为他长远的好处，承担引导与纠正的辛劳——哪怕当下不讨喜。这原则也适用于我们对待自己的软弱：有时爱自己，就是不再放任。',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 23:13–14 says not to withhold correction out of soft affection — fitting discipline actually rescues a child. In an age that easily equates “discipline” with “harm,” this needs careful reading: Scripture never endorses abuse or venting anger on a child; it speaks of loving, measured, principled guidance. Real love isn’t letting a child do whatever they want; it’s the willingness to bear the hard work of guiding and correcting for their long-term good — even when it isn’t popular in the moment. The same principle applies to how we handle our own weaknesses: sometimes loving yourself means no longer letting things slide.' },
    { tag: 'ADDICTION', tagZh: '成瘾', verseRef: 'Proverbs 23:35',
      titleZh: '当享受开始替你做决定', titleEn: 'When a pleasure starts making your decisions',
      bodyZh: '箴言 23:29–35 用惊人的写实，描绘了一个被酒辖制的人：先是舒畅，继而失去清醒，到最后连挨打都感觉不到，醒来第一个念头却是“我仍去寻酒”。这幅画像的力量，在于它照见了一切成瘾的共同轨迹——不只是酒，也可能是手机、赌博、色情、购物或别的。判断的信号是：它是否开始替你做决定？你是否一再对自己说“最后一次”？如果是，这不是意志力薄弱的羞耻，而是一个可以寻求帮助的处境。承认，是走向自由的第一步。',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 23:29–35 gives a startlingly realistic portrait of a person mastered by drink: first the pleasant glow, then lost clarity, until he no longer even feels the blows — and his first thought on waking is, “I will look for more.” The power of this picture is that it exposes the common arc of every addiction — not only alcohol, but the phone, gambling, pornography, shopping, and more. The telling sign: has it started making your decisions? Do you keep telling yourself “last time”? If so, this isn’t a shame about weak willpower; it’s a situation where you can seek help. Admitting it is the first step toward freedom.' },
  ],

